<div class="entry-content single-content">
	
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">What size rug do you need? This free <strong>rug size calculator</strong> works out the right rug for a living room, dining room, bedroom or hallway from a few quick measurements, and shows the closest standard size to buy.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It works in feet and inches or centimeters and gives US sizes (like 8&#8242; x 10&#8242;) or UK sizes (like 160 x 230 cm). A typical living room is filled in, so change the numbers to match your room and the result updates as you type.</p>



<div id="dsh-rug" class="dsh-calc">

<h3>What size rug do you need?</h3>
<p class="sub">Pick the room, enter a few measurements and get the rug size that fits, plus the closest standard size to buy.</p>
<div class="row">
<div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Where you shop"><button type="button" data-r="us">US sizes</button><button type="button" data-r="uk">UK sizes</button></div>
<div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Units"><button type="button" data-u="in">feet and inches</button><button type="button" data-u="cm">cm</button></div>
</div>
<fieldset><legend>1. The room</legend>
<div class="opts" role="group" aria-label="Room">
<button type="button" data-m="living" class="on"><b>Living room</b><span>Sofa and coffee table</span></button>
<button type="button" data-m="dining"><b>Dining room</b><span>Rug under the table</span></button>
<button type="button" data-m="bed"><b>Bedroom</b><span>Rug under the bed</span></button>
<button type="button" data-m="hall"><b>Hallway</b><span>A runner</span></button>
</div>
<div class="grid">
<label><span id="lrl">Room length (wall to wall)</span><span data-lu="ft"></span><input type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="0.5" id="rl"></label>
<label><span id="lrw">Room width (wall to wall)</span><span data-lu="ft"></span><input type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="0.5" id="rw"></label>
</div></fieldset>
<fieldset id="f-living"><legend>2. Your seating</legend>
<div class="grid"><label>Sofa length<span data-lu="in"></span><input type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="0.5" id="sl"></label></div>
<p class="hint">You will get three options: every leg on the rug, front legs on the rug, or a smaller rug under the coffee table only.</p>
</fieldset>
<fieldset id="f-dining" class="hide"><legend>2. Your table</legend>
<div class="opts" role="group" aria-label="Table shape">
<button type="button" data-t="rect" class="on"><b>Rectangular or square</b></button>
<button type="button" data-t="round"><b>Round</b></button>
</div>
<div class="grid">
<label><span id="ltl">Table length</span><span data-lu="in"></span><input type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="0.5" id="tl"></label>
<label id="ltw">Table width<span data-lu="in"></span><input type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="0.5" id="tw"></label>
<label>Chairs<select id="cc"><option value="61">Slim chairs, tight room (24 in / 61 cm each side)</option><option value="76" selected>Standard chairs (30 in / 76 cm each side)</option><option value="91">Large or upholstered chairs (36 in / 91 cm each side)</option></select></label>
</div></fieldset>
<fieldset id="f-bed" class="hide"><legend>2. Your bed</legend>
<div class="grid">
<label>Bed size<select id="bs"></select></label>
<label>Rug past the bed, each side<select id="be"><option value="61">At least 24 in (61 cm)</option><option value="76" selected>About 30 in (76 cm)</option><option value="91">About 36 in (91 cm), big rooms</option></select></label>
<label>Placement<select id="bp"><option value="23" selected>Under the lower two-thirds of the bed</option><option value="full">Under the whole bed</option></select></label>
</div></fieldset>
<fieldset id="f-hall" class="hide"><legend>2. Your hallway</legend>
<p class="hint">Use the room length and width above for the hallway. The runner stops about 5 in (13 cm) short of each wall and end.</p>
</fieldset>
<div class="res" aria-live="polite">
<div id="wn"></div>
<div id="vd" class="verdict"></div>
<div class="kpis">
<div class="kpi"><small id="k1t">Ideal rug size</small><strong id="k1"></strong><em id="k1e"></em></div>
<div class="kpi"><small>Largest that fits</small><strong id="k2"></strong><em id="k2e"></em></div>
<div class="kpi"><small>Best standard size</small><strong id="k3"></strong><em id="k3e"></em></div>
</div>
<div class="wrap"><div class="buy"><h4>Your options</h4><div id="by"></div></div><div class="viz" id="vz" aria-hidden="true"></div></div>
<ul class="tips" id="tp"></ul>
<div class="acts"><button type="button" class="pri" id="cp">Copy my rug size</button><button type="button" id="pr">Print</button></div>
<p class="note">Rules used: about 18 in (46 cm) of bare floor between the rug and the walls where the room allows, and never less than about 12 in (30 cm) in a living room or bedroom; dining rugs extend 24 to 36 in (61 to 91 cm) past each side of the table so pulled-out chairs stay on the rug; bedroom rugs extend at least 24 in (61 cm) past the sides of the bed; runners stop about 5 in (13 cm) from the walls and ends. Standard sizes vary by shop. Results are planning estimates.</p>
</div>

</div>




<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Choose the Right Rug Size</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The most common mistake is a rug that is too small. According to the <a href="https://contentgrid.homedepot-static.com/hdus/en_US/DTCCOMNEW/fetch/Global_Assets/PDFs/Selecting-Proper-Rug-Size.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Home Depot rug size guide</a>, the right size depends on how the furniture is arranged, not just on the size of the room. Measure the furniture group first, then check that the rug still leaves a border of bare floor to the walls.</p>



<figure class="dsh-diagram"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 1200 900" font-family="Poppins, sans-serif" role="img" aria-labelledby="rg-t rg-d"><title id="rg-t">Rug size rules by room</title><desc id="rg-d">Four rooms drawn from above showing how big a rug should be. Living room: the rug sits under the front legs of the sofa and chairs, with about 18 to 24 inches of bare floor to the walls. Dining room: the rug extends 24 to 36 inches past every side of the table so pulled-out chairs stay on it. Bedroom: the rug lies across the lower two-thirds of the bed and extends at least 24 inches past each side. Hallway: a runner stops about 4 to 6 inches from each wall and each end.</desc><rect width="1200" height="900" fill="#F7F1E9"/><text x="60" y="88" font-size="44" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">Rug Size Rules by Room</text><text x="60" y="130" font-size="22" fill="#6E645C">Seen from above. Size the rug to the furniture, then check the border.</text><text x="1140" y="60" font-size="18" fill="#6E645C" text-anchor="end">dailyspacehacks.com</text><text x="60" y="184" font-size="24" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">Living room</text><rect x="60" y="200" width="500" height="290" fill="#fff" stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="5"/><text x="60" y="524" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#C85A32">Front legs on the rug, 18–24 in of floor to the walls</text><rect x="120" y="250" width="380" height="190" fill="#DCE5D6" stroke="#8FA689" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="10 6"/><rect x="170" y="410" width="280" height="70" rx="12" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="250" y="310" width="120" height="60" rx="8" fill="#D9B48A"/><rect x="155" y="230" width="70" height="60" rx="10" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="395" y="230" width="70" height="60" rx="10" fill="#B98A5E"/><line x1="60" y1="220" x2="120" y2="220" stroke="#C85A32" stroke-width="3"/><text x="126" y="226" font-size="15" fill="#C85A32" font-weight="700">border</text><text x="640" y="184" font-size="24" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">Dining room</text><rect x="640" y="200" width="500" height="290" fill="#fff" stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="5"/><text x="640" y="524" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#C85A32">24–36 in of rug past every side of the table</text><rect x="690" y="230" width="400" height="230" fill="#DCE5D6" stroke="#8FA689" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="10 6"/><rect x="790" y="305" width="200" height="80" rx="6" fill="#D9B48A"/><rect x="805" y="262" width="40" height="36" rx="6" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="805" y="392" width="40" height="36" rx="6" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="870" y="262" width="40" height="36" rx="6" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="870" y="392" width="40" height="36" rx="6" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="935" y="262" width="40" height="36" rx="6" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="935" y="392" width="40" height="36" rx="6" fill="#B98A5E"/><line x1="990" y1="345" x2="1090" y2="345" stroke="#C85A32" stroke-width="3"/><text x="1040" y="337" font-size="16" font-weight="700" fill="#C85A32" text-anchor="middle">24–36 in</text><text x="60" y="569" font-size="24" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">Bedroom</text><rect x="60" y="585" width="500" height="235" fill="#fff" stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="5"/><text x="60" y="854" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#C85A32">At least 24 in past each side, under the lower two-thirds</text><rect x="150" y="645" width="320" height="165" fill="#DCE5D6" stroke="#8FA689" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="10 6"/><rect x="235" y="591" width="150" height="174" rx="10" fill="#B98A5E" opacity=".95"/><rect x="245" y="599" width="130" height="40" rx="8" fill="#F3EDE4"/><rect x="170" y="593" width="50" height="40" rx="4" fill="#D9B48A"/><rect x="400" y="593" width="50" height="40" rx="4" fill="#D9B48A"/><line x1="385" y1="715" x2="470" y2="715" stroke="#C85A32" stroke-width="3"/><text x="428" y="707" font-size="16" font-weight="700" fill="#C85A32" text-anchor="middle">24 in+</text><text x="640" y="644" font-size="24" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">Hallway runner</text><rect x="640" y="660" width="500" height="110" fill="#fff" stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="5"/><text x="640" y="804" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#C85A32">4–6 in of floor at each side and end</text><rect x="662" y="682" width="456" height="66" fill="#DCE5D6" stroke="#8FA689" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="10 6"/><line x1="1118" y1="715.0" x2="1140" y2="715.0" stroke="#C85A32" stroke-width="3"/><text x="1114" y="707.0" font-size="15" fill="#C85A32" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">4–6 in</text><text x="60" y="892" font-size="14" fill="#6E645C">Rules of thumb from The Home Depot rug size guide and Fibreworks custom rug guide. Measure your own furniture before you buy.</text></svg><figcaption>Rug size rules by room: front legs on the rug in a living room, 24 to 36 in of rug past every side of a dining table, at least 24 in past each side of a bed and 4 to 6 in of floor around a hallway runner.</figcaption></figure>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Measure the room</strong> from wall to wall, and note doors, vents and fireplaces.</li>


<li><strong>Measure the furniture group</strong>: the sofa, the dining table or the bed.</li>


<li><strong>Choose the look</strong>: all legs on the rug, front legs only, or a smaller rug under a coffee table.</li>


<li><strong>Mark it out with painter&#8217;s tape</strong> and live with it for a day before you buy.</li>
</ol>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1536" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/rug-size-calculator-01-living-room.jpg" alt="Rug size calculator: a living room with a large cream and sage rug under the front legs of the sofa and armchairs, a round coffee table on the rug and a border of bare oak floor to the walls" class="wp-image-2399" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/rug-size-calculator-01-living-room.jpg 1536w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/rug-size-calculator-01-living-room-300x200.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/rug-size-calculator-01-living-room-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/rug-size-calculator-01-living-room-768x512.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1536px) 100vw, 1536px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Living Room Rug Size</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <a href="https://contentgrid.homedepot-static.com/hdus/en_US/DTCCOMNEW/fetch/Global_Assets/PDFs/Selecting-Proper-Rug-Size.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Home Depot guide</a> shows three ways to place a living room rug. Our rug size calculator gives you all three:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Layout</th><th>How it looks</th><th>Typical size</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>All legs on the rug</td><td>Every piece of seating sits on the rug, with about 12 to 18 in (30 to 46 cm) of floor to the walls</td><td>10&#8242; x 13&#8242; or larger (280 x 360 cm)</td></tr><tr><td>Front legs on the rug</td><td>The rug tucks under the front legs of the sofa and chairs</td><td>8&#8242; x 10&#8242; or larger (200 x 290 cm)</td></tr><tr><td>Coffee table only</td><td>A smaller rug under the coffee table, about as long as the sofa</td><td>At least 5&#8242; x 8&#8242; (160 x 230 cm)</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Front legs on the rug is the easiest choice in most rooms: it connects the sofa and chairs without needing a huge rug, and it suits a sofa that stands against a wall. In a small living room, a rug under the coffee table works, but it should still be at least 5&#8242; x 8&#8242; so it does not look like a doormat.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Dining Room Rug Size</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A dining rug must be large enough that chairs stay on it when you pull them out. The <a href="https://fibreworks.com/pages/custom-size-rug-guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fibreworks custom rug guide</a> suggests measuring the table and adding 24 to 36 in (61 to 91 cm) to each side, with 30 in (76 cm) as the sweet spot and 36 in for large upholstered chairs. The rug size calculator uses the chair setting you choose.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Table size</th><th>Rug needed (30 in each side)</th><th>Standard size to buy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>36 x 60 in (4 seats)</td><td>96 x 120 in</td><td>8&#8242; x 10&#8242;</td></tr><tr><td>40 x 72 in (6 seats)</td><td>100 x 132 in</td><td>9&#8242; x 12&#8242;</td></tr><tr><td>42 x 84 in (8 seats)</td><td>102 x 144 in</td><td>9&#8242; x 12&#8242; (snug) or 10&#8242; x 13&#8242;</td></tr><tr><td>48 in round</td><td>108 in round</td><td>10&#8242; round, or a square rug</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/rug-size-calculator-02-dining-room.jpg" alt="Dining table and six chairs on a large jute rug that extends well past the table so pulled-out chairs stay on the rug, with bare floor around it" class="wp-image-2401" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/rug-size-calculator-02-dining-room.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/rug-size-calculator-02-dining-room-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/rug-size-calculator-02-dining-room-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/rug-size-calculator-02-dining-room-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Bedroom Rug Size</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In a bedroom, the rug should show on both sides of the bed and at the foot. The <a href="https://contentgrid.homedepot-static.com/hdus/en_US/DTCCOMNEW/fetch/Global_Assets/PDFs/Selecting-Proper-Rug-Size.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Home Depot guide</a> recommends that it extend at least 24 in (61 cm) from the edges of the bed, and the <a href="https://fibreworks.com/pages/custom-size-rug-guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fibreworks guide</a> suggests 30 to 36 in (76 to 91 cm) on each side and at the foot, with the rug under at least the lower two-thirds of the bed.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Bed</th><th>Rug under the lower two-thirds</th><th>Rug under the whole bed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Twin</td><td>7&#8242; 6&#8243; x 9&#8242; 6&#8243; (6&#8242; x 9&#8242; if space is tight)</td><td>8&#8242; x 10&#8242;</td></tr><tr><td>Full</td><td>7&#8242; 6&#8243; x 9&#8242; 6&#8243; or 8&#8242; x 10&#8242;</td><td>9&#8242; x 12&#8242;</td></tr><tr><td>Queen</td><td>8&#8242; x 10&#8242;</td><td>9&#8242; x 12&#8242;</td></tr><tr><td>King</td><td>9&#8242; x 12&#8242;</td><td>9&#8242; x 12&#8242; or 10&#8242; x 13&#8242;</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Stop the rug just short of the nightstands so they stand level on the floor. If the room is too small for a large rug, a runner on each side of the bed is a good alternative.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/rug-size-calculator-03-bedroom.jpg" alt="Queen bed with a large cream rug laid crosswise under the lower part of the bed, showing at the side and foot, with nightstands on the bare floor" class="wp-image-2402" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/rug-size-calculator-03-bedroom.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/rug-size-calculator-03-bedroom-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/rug-size-calculator-03-bedroom-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/rug-size-calculator-03-bedroom-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Hallway Runner Size</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For a hallway, the <a href="https://fibreworks.com/pages/custom-size-rug-guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fibreworks guide</a> recommends leaving about 4 to 6 in (10 to 15 cm) of floor between the runner and each wall and each end of the hallway. Fibreworks says a 30 in (76 cm) wide runner suits most hallways; common US lengths are 8, 10 and 12 ft. For a long hallway, use two runners in a row or order a custom length, and keep the runner clear of doors so they can open.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Standard Rug Sizes</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Rug sizes are not quite the same in the US and the UK. These are common sizes from the <a href="https://contentgrid.homedepot-static.com/hdus/en_US/DTCCOMNEW/fetch/Global_Assets/PDFs/Selecting-Proper-Rug-Size.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Home Depot guide</a> and the UK retailer <a href="https://www.ruglove.co.uk/pages/rug-sizes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rug Love</a>. Exact sizes vary by brand, so check the label.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>US size</th><th>In cm</th><th>Nearest UK size</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3&#8242; x 5&#8242;</td><td>91 x 152 cm</td><td>80 x 150 cm</td></tr><tr><td>4&#8242; x 6&#8242;</td><td>122 x 183 cm</td><td>120 x 170 cm</td></tr><tr><td>5&#8242; x 8&#8242;</td><td>152 x 244 cm</td><td>160 x 230 cm</td></tr><tr><td>8&#8242; x 10&#8242;</td><td>244 x 305 cm</td><td>200 x 290 cm</td></tr><tr><td>9&#8242; x 12&#8242;</td><td>274 x 366 cm</td><td>280 x 360 cm</td></tr><tr><td>10&#8242; x 13&#8242;</td><td>305 x 396 cm</td><td>280 x 360 cm or custom</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Rug Size FAQ</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What size rug do I need for my living room?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For most living rooms, an 8&#8242; x 10&#8242; rug (or 200 x 290 cm in the UK) lets the front legs of the sofa and chairs rest on it. In a large room, go up to 9&#8242; x 12&#8242; or 10&#8242; x 13&#8242; so every leg fits, and keep about 12 to 18 in (30 to 46 cm) of floor showing to the walls.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How do you calculate rug size for a room?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Measure the room and subtract 12 to 18 in (30 to 46 cm) from each side to leave a border of floor; that is the largest rug for an all-legs-on look. Then check the furniture: the rug should be a little longer than the sofa, or 24 to 36 in (61 to 91 cm) bigger than the dining table on every side. The rug size calculator above does both checks for you.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What size rug goes under a queen bed?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">An 8&#8242; x 10&#8242; rug laid crosswise under the lower two-thirds of a queen bed leaves about 30 in (76 cm) of rug on each side. For a rug under the whole bed, or in a bigger room, choose 9&#8242; x 12&#8242;.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Is it better to have a rug too big or too small?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Slightly too big is usually better. A rug that is too small makes furniture look like it is floating and the room feel smaller. Just keep a border of bare floor around the edges so the rug does not run wall to wall.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">More Planning Tools</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Planning the rest of the room? Try our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/curtain-size-calculator/">curtain size calculator</a> for the windows, the <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/moving-box-calculator/">moving box calculator</a> if you are moving, and our ideas for <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-bedroom-storage-ideas/">small bedroom storage</a> and <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/room-divider-ideas/">dividing a room without a wall</a>.</p>



